  "worked_solution": "First, we drop the parentheses and the addition sign, which gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we put the fractions over a common denominator of $9$ and combine the numerators, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{9} & =\\frac{6}{9}-\\frac{4}{9}=\\frac{6-4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we work out $6-4$ using our number line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14149424/assets/4ebf100b7da9bbada9145b4b5ea018cfee8ff2037307ca5023d6c2de54705fdd.png)\n\nWe find that $6-4=2.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{6-4}{9}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$",
